    "text": "Esus was a Gaulish god, also called Hesus. His name may mean strength, rapid motion, or anger. The Roman writer Lucan called him uncouth Esus of the barbarous altars. Druids read the future from the blood of victims hung in trees. Other authors tied Esus to Mercury and Mars. He served merchants and warriors. Altars show Esus with bulls and cranes or as a woodcutter. The Breton town of Essé and the people Esuvii carry his name. Personal names include Esugenos, the son of Esus, and Esunertus, strong as Esus. His victims hung in trees were hung and he cut wood. Koch's Paris stone in the carved wood of the bull ruled beside a tree.",
